{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Instructor Toolkit for CAT 531", "title": "Discussion Prompts by Module", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting discussion prompts by module to TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "08", "section": "05", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531", "cat531_supplement_discussion_prompts_by_module", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/08/04"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_prompts_by_module_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_prompts_by_module_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_prompts_by_module_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_prompts_by_module_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 5, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Discussion Prompts by Module\n\n![Discussion Prompts by Module textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/08-05-discussion-prompts-by-module.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA discussion prompt is an instructional instrument, and most online discussions are weak because the prompt was weak. Ask \"is AI good or bad for the classroom?\" and you get a wall of one-line opinions that never touch each other. The skill in this section is designing prompts that force a concrete decision, name a genuine tension to negotiate, require evidence, and oblige students to engage a peer who chose differently. A well-built prompt does the pedagogical work before anyone posts; a poorly built one cannot be rescued by good facilitation.\n\nThis matters because discussion is where teacher candidates rehearse the judgment the course is trying to build. If the prompt lets them restate a stance, they practice nothing. If the prompt makes them weigh competing goods and commit with evidence, they practice exactly the defensible reasoning they will need when they make real technology decisions in their own classrooms.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA facilitator posts the Module 3 prompt: \"Is AI good or bad for the classroom? Post your opinion.\" The board fills with thirty posts that agree with each other and cite nothing. No one has to choose, no one has to weigh a cost, and no one has to respond to a classmate. The discussion looks active and teaches little. The question this section answers is what to change in the prompt itself so the same students produce reasoned, evidence-backed, mutually engaged argument.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA productive discussion prompt is built from four design components, and a prompt that omits any of them tends to collapse into opinion exchange:\n\n- **A concrete decision.** Replace the abstract valence question (\"good or bad?\") with a specific choice a teacher would actually face, attached to a named person and situation. A decision creates stakes that an opinion does not.\n- **A named tension.** State the two competing goods the student must negotiate, such as access versus a shared common text. Without a tension there is nothing to deliberate, only something to assert.\n- **An evidence requirement.** Oblige students to cite at least one course anchor and one detail from the case, so posts rest on reasoning rather than gut feeling.\n- **A reply obligation that engages disagreement.** Require a response to a peer who made the opposite call, which forces students to take other reasoning seriously rather than talk past it.\n\nThe facilitator's own judgment shows in what gets cut as much as in what gets added. A generic follow-up like \"what does everyone think?\" reopens opinion and undoes the tension the prompt worked to create, so a strong design records that such a move was deliberately rejected. Critical edtech studies helps you choose tensions that are real (often involving who is burdened or excluded), and TPACK keeps the decision tied to genuine content and pedagogy rather than to novelty.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare a closed prompt that produces thin posts with a designed prompt that produces deliberation.\n\n### Annotated judgment call: a discussion prompt that opens judgment instead of closing it\n\nScenario: a facilitator is writing the module-3 discussion prompt for CAT 531. The first draft is a yes-or-no question that yields one-line posts; the revised prompt requires students to weigh a tension and commit to a position with evidence.\n\nBefore (closed prompt that produces thin posts):\n\n> Discussion prompt (Module 3): Is AI good or bad for the classroom? Post your opinion.\n\nWalk the prompt against what good discussion judgment requires:\n\n1. Decision check. \"Good or bad\" has no decision in it; students restate a stance with no constraint to wrestle. A strong prompt names a specific decision a teacher would actually face.\n2. Evidence check. The before prompt asks for opinion, so nothing requires students to cite a course anchor or a case detail.\n3. Tension check. There is no value conflict to negotiate, so posts do not have to engage anyone else's reasoning.\n\nAfter (annotated discussion prompt):\n\n> Discussion prompt (Module 3, 08.05): A 4th-grade teacher wants to use an AI reading-level rewriter so every student gets the same article at their own level. Name the value conflict (access versus shared common text), take a position on whether she should use it for next week's lesson, and justify it with one course anchor (TPACK or critical edtech studies) and one detail from the case. Then reply to a peer who chose the opposite call. Facilitator note: I rejected the auto-suggested follow-up \"What does everyone think?\" because it reopens opinion instead of pressing on the tension.\n\nThe after prompt is stronger because it forces a concrete decision, names a real tension to negotiate, requires evidence, and the facilitator annotation shows one suggested move that was deliberately rejected.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Pose a concrete decision a real teacher would face (the 4th-grade AI reading-level rewriter for next week)\", \"Name the value conflict to negotiate (access for every reader versus a shared common text)\", \"Require evidence: one course anchor (TPACK or critical edtech studies) plus one detail from the case\", \"Oblige a reply to a peer who made the opposite call\", \"Record the rejected move (cut the open follow-up that reopens mere opinion)\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A colleague wants to add the line 'What does everyone think?' to the end of your designed prompt to boost participation. Explain why a strong design records this as a rejected move rather than adding it.\" answer=\"An open 'what does everyone think?' reopens opinion and dissolves the tension the prompt worked to create, returning the thread to one-line stances that engage no one. Volume of posts is not the goal; reasoned, evidence-backed, mutually engaged argument is. Recording the rejection shows the facilitator's judgment about what protects deliberation, and it documents that the cut was deliberate rather than an oversight.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen designing a discussion prompt, build in the concrete decision first, the named tension second, and the evidence-and-reply requirement third. Before you post a prompt, confirm it answers:\n\n1. **Decision check:** Does the prompt require a specific choice a teacher would actually face, not a stance on an abstraction?\n2. **Tension check:** Are two competing goods named so students have something genuine to negotiate?\n3. **Evidence-and-engagement check:** Must students cite an anchor and a case detail, and must they respond to a peer who disagreed?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\n<inline-check question=\"A Module 5 draft asks: 'Should schools protect student data? Reply to two classmates.' Why will this prompt produce thin posts despite the reply requirement?\" explanation=\"The decision is a foregone conclusion (everyone agrees schools should protect data), so there is no real tension to negotiate and no opposite call for a reply to engage. A reply obligation cannot rescue a prompt with no genuine value conflict.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The reply requirement guarantees deep engagement regardless of the question.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The decision has no genuine tension, so there is no opposite position for peers to engage.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It cites too many course anchors.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is too specific about the data scenario.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe most common error is judging a prompt by the volume of posts it generates. A bad prompt can produce many shallow posts; the measure is whether students had to reason and engage each other, not how full the board looks. A second error is adding a warm, open follow-up to \"encourage participation,\" which usually dissolves the tension and returns the thread to opinion. A third is naming a fake tension, two options that are not really in conflict, which lets students pick the obvious side without deliberation.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nSuppose you are writing the Module 5 prompt on data privacy. Resist \"should schools protect student data?\" because everyone agrees. Instead give a decision: a teacher can adopt a free analytics dashboard that improves feedback but shares anonymized clickstream data with the vendor. Name the tension as better feedback versus student data exposure. Require one anchor and one case detail. Require a reply to someone who chose the opposite. Then check your draft for any sentence that lets students escape into general opinion, and cut it the way the facilitator cut \"what does everyone think?\"\n\nThe artifact is the designed prompt plus a short facilitator note recording at least one move you rejected and why.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead a discussion prompt by predicting the posts it will produce. If you can imagine a satisfying post that cites nothing and engages no one, the prompt is too open. Check that the decision is concrete, the tension is real, and the reply obligation forces engagement with disagreement. Keep a two-column note: the design component on the left, the exact sentence in the prompt that delivers it on the right. A strong prompt has a sentence for each component and no sentence that reopens mere opinion.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA discussion prompt meets the standard when a mentor teacher can see the concrete decision, the named tension, the evidence requirement, and a recorded rejected move. Wording polish is the last layer; the design components are what carry the prompt. If a component is missing, annotate the gap and ask BigAL to compare your prompt against an exemplar that forces deliberation rather than to make it sound more inviting. A useful closing line reads: \"I built in a concrete decision and a real tension with an evidence and reply requirement, and I rejected the open follow-up because it would have returned the thread to opinion.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the prompt design to a synchronous, in-class debate rather than an asynchronous board. The components transfer: a concrete decision, a real tension, an evidence requirement. The reply obligation becomes a structured rebuttal round, and the rejected move might be an open \"any other thoughts?\" that lets the energy dissipate. Rebuilding the prompt for the live format, rather than pasting the board version into a slide, shows that you understand prompt design as a transferable instrument for deliberation.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/08-05-discussion-prompts-by-module-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"08.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **TPACK**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the TeachGen@i prompt transcript with teacher edits and rejected suggestions for CAT 531 08.05. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_discussion_prompts_by_module\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\", \"cat531_supplement_discussion_prompts_by_module\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your prompt forces a concrete decision, names a real tension, requires an anchor and a case detail, obliges a reply to a dissenting peer, and records one move you rejected.\n\n## References\n\n- TPACK. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- AI and education datafication. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_prompts_by_module_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_discussion_prompts_by_module", "stem": "A module discussion prompt reads: \"Is AI good or bad for the classroom? Post your opinion.\" Why does this prompt produce thin posts?", "options": ["It is too long for students to read.", "It contains no decision to make; students restate a stance with no specific tension to wrestle with.", "It mentions AI, which students dislike.", "It asks for a post instead of an essay."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A closed good-or-bad question has no decision in it, so students just restate an opinion.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_closed_opinion_prompt", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531", "cat531_supplement_discussion_prompts_by_module", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_prompts_by_module_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "To make discussion posts grounded rather than just felt, what should the prompt require?", "options": ["A minimum word count.", "One course anchor (such as TPACK or critical edtech studies) and one detail from the case.", "A personal story about technology.", "A summary of the reading."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Requiring a course anchor and a case detail forces evidence-based reasoning instead of opinion.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_no_evidence_required", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_discussion_prompts_by_module", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_prompts_by_module_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "A facilitator drafting a prompt about an AI reading-level rewriter is offered the auto-suggested follow-up \"What does everyone think?\" What is the defensible move?", "options": ["Accept it, since more responses are always better.", "Reject it: it reopens opinion and drops the named tension (access versus shared common text) the prompt was built around.", "Accept it but shorten it.", "Let the tool decide whether to add it."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A generic follow-up reopens opinion instead of pressing the tension, so the facilitator rejects it.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_reopening_opinion", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_discussion_prompts_by_module", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_prompts_by_module_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "Which addition most increases the chance that students engage one another's reasoning rather than post in isolation?", "options": ["Tell students to make their posts longer.", "Require each student to reply to a peer who took the opposite position on the decision.", "Grade only the first post.", "Ask students to like three other posts."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Requiring a reply to a peer who chose the opposite call forces students to engage reasoning that differs from their own.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_no_peer_engagement", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_discussion_prompts_by_module", "evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_closed_opinion_prompt", "pattern": "writing_a_closed_opinion_prompt", "description": "Writing a discussion prompt like \"Is AI good or bad?\" that asks for an opinion with no decision to make, producing one-line posts", "trigger": "prompt has no decision, tension, or case for students to engage", "feedback": "Name a specific decision a teacher would face. A good prompt has a tension to negotiate, not a stance to restate.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_no_evidence_required", "pattern": "prompt_that_requires_no_evidence", "description": "Asking only for opinion so nothing requires students to cite a course anchor or a case detail", "trigger": "prompt asks what students think with no evidence requirement", "feedback": "Require a course anchor and a case detail so the post is grounded, not just felt.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_no_peer_engagement", "pattern": "prompt_with_no_peer_engagement", "description": "Writing a prompt where students post in isolation and never have to engage a peer who reasoned differently", "trigger": "prompt ends after the first post with no reply requirement", "feedback": "Require a reply to a peer who chose the opposite call so students engage other reasoning.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_8_5_discussion_reopening_opinion", "pattern": "follow_up_that_reopens_opinion", "description": "Adding a generic follow-up such as \"What does everyone think?\" that reopens opinion instead of pressing on the tension", "trigger": "facilitator accepts a vague follow-up that drops the named tension", "feedback": "Reject follow-ups that reopen opinion. Press the same tension or escalate the decision.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}